thinking_content: '**Waiting and Planning: A Reflection on the SOTA Replication**


  Alright, the submission''s running. Now''s the perfect time to clear my head and formulate a plan. I''ve sent in my replication of that SOTA model, and frankly, I''m expecting the results to be very close to the original. I''m hoping for something around 0.7404.


  But what if it''s not? That''s where things get interesting. I''ll need to meticulously go through the details. Slight variations in parameters, differences in floating-point precision, `rng_seed` usage, even the implicit behavior of `scanpy`—these seemingly minor elements can lead to significant discrepancies. I''ll have to consider all of these things if the scores are significantly different.


  Assuming a successful replication, this sets a really strong foundation for my own explorations. I can immediately jump into ablation studies, systematically tweaking the model. Removing Batch-Robust HVG, experimenting with variance weighting, simplifying the density adaptation, and playing with the ridge regression parameters - these are just the initial ideas that come to mind. I''ll also start tuning hyperparameters like `k_total`, `k_density`, `delta`, `lam_override`, `alpha_g`, and `alpha_emb`. It would also be great if I can combine this with my earlier work. If the SOTA embeddings and my BBKNN-like approach can be combined without interference, that would be a bonus!


  If the replication fails, though, it''ll be a deep dive into the code. Line-by-line comparison with the original SOTA implementation will be crucial. I''m ready to debug, but I''m keeping my fingers crossed for a successful run. Now, let''s head into the reflection chamber.

  '
