parts:
- text: "I will continue drafting the research paper, focusing on refining the \"Results\" section, presenting the verifiable data and a comparative analysis.\n\n---\n\n**3. Results:**\n\nThe three selected batch integration strategies were evaluated on the Human Heart Dataset using 10 OpenProblems 2.0 metrics. The normalized scores, ranging from 0 to 1, are summarized in Table 1.\n\n**Table 1: Comparative Performance of Batch Integration Methods on Human Heart Dataset**\n\n| Metric | SOTA Replication (ID 1288) | Scanpy Combat (ID 1231) | Manual BBKNN-like Graph (ID 1267) |\n|:----------------|:----------------------------:|:-------------------------:|:----------------------------------:|\n| **Overall Score** | **0.7415**                   | 0.6008                    | 0.4106                             |\n| ASW_batch       | 0.659                        | 0.664                     | 0.000 (NaN)                        |\n| ASW_label       | 0.248                        | 0.223          \
    \           | 0.000 (NaN)                        |\n| ARI             | 0.765                        | 0.593                     | 0.726                              |\n| NMI             | 0.722                        | 0.694                     | 0.689                              |\n| Graph_conn      | 0.967                        | 0.877                     | 0.925                              |\n| kBET            | 0.789                        | 0.612                     | 0.000 (NaN)                        |\n| iLISI           | 0.965                        | 0.035                     | 0.850                              |\n| cLISI           | 0.973                        | 0.986                     | 0.916                              |\n| PCR             | 1.000                        | 1.000                     | 0.000 (NaN)                        |\n| Cell_cycle      | 0.327                        | 0.323                     | 0.000 (NaN)                        |\n*(Note: NaN\
    \ values from the evaluation system are normalized to 0.0 for overall score calculation.)*\n\n**3.1. SOTA Replication Performance (ID 1288):**\nThe full replication of Daedalus IV's Synergy Model (ID 1288) achieved an overall score of **0.7415**, successfully reproducing the State-of-the-Art performance (Daedalus IV ID 1273 score: 0.7404). This method demonstrated a highly balanced performance across all evaluation metrics. Notably, it achieved:\n*   Exceptional local batch mixing (iLISI: 0.965), addressing a critical challenge for many integration methods.\n*   Strong preservation of global biological structure (PCR: 1.000, cLISI: 0.973).\n*   High accuracy in cell type clustering and agreement (ARI: 0.765, NMI: 0.722).\n*   Robust graph connectivity (Graph_conn: 0.967) and effective batch mixing within neighborhoods (kBET: 0.789).\nThis comprehensive performance confirms the SOTA model's ability to simultaneously address multiple facets of batch integration.\n\n**3.2. Comparative Analysis\
    \ with Baselines:**\n\n**Scanpy Combat Integration (ID 1231):** With an overall score of 0.6008, this baseline performed reasonably well but exhibited significant limitations.\n*   **Strengths:** Achieved excellent global batch removal (ASW_batch: 0.664) and robust preservation of principal components (PCR: 1.000, cLISI: 0.986). These metrics suggest effective global alignment of batches.\n*   **Weaknesses:** Critically, Combat demonstrated very poor local batch mixing (iLISI: 0.035). This indicates that while batch effects might be removed globally, local neighborhoods still largely consist of cells from a single batch, hindering fine-grained biological analysis and downstream tasks. Other biological preservation metrics like ARI (0.593) and NMI (0.694) were moderate.\n\n**Manual BBKNN-like Graph Construction (ID 1267):** This graph-only output method yielded a low overall score of 0.4106 but provided key insights into local integration.\n*   **Strengths:** As hypothesized, this method\
    \ achieved a very high local batch mixing score (iLISI: 0.850). It also showed strong performance in clustering agreement (ARI: 0.726, NMI: 0.689) and graph connectivity (Graph_conn: 0.925). These results validate the efficacy of batch-aware graph construction for improving local integration.\n*   **Weaknesses:** Due to explicitly returning only the graph (without a dense embedding), several metrics dependent on embeddings (ASW_batch, ASW_label, kBET, PCR, Cell_cycle) could not be computed and were scored as 0.0. This significantly depressed its overall score, highlighting the necessity of a comprehensive output (both embedding and graph) for a balanced evaluation.\n\n**Trade-offs and SOTA's Balance:**\nThe comparative analysis reveals distinct trade-offs inherent in different integration strategies. Combat excels at global batch effect removal and maintaining global structure (high ASW_batch, PCR), but struggles with local mixing (low iLISI). Conversely, graph-based approaches (like\
    \ my Manual BBKNN-like method) can achieve high local mixing (high iLISI) but may sacrifice global metrics if not carefully implemented or if they fail to provide a suitable embedding. The SOTA model effectively resolves these trade-offs by employing a **dual-path strategy**. It leverages a Combat-like process for embedding-based metrics and a sophisticated, density-adaptive graph construction for graph-based metrics like iLISI, allowing it to achieve strong performance across both global and local integration aspects simultaneously, as evidenced by its high overall score and robust individual metric values.\n\n---\nThis \"Results\" section now provides a clear, data-driven account of my experiments, directly addressing the reviewer's feedback. I have presented a table, detailed the SOTA replication, compared it to baselines, and analyzed the observed trade-offs.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Continue drafting the research paper. Focus on writing the \"Discussion\"\
    \ and \"Conclusion and Future Work\" sections. In the discussion, interpret the results, reflect on my research journey (my prior struggles vs. SOTA success), and tie into station context (stagnation). In the conclusion, summarize findings and outline concrete future research directions based on the SOTA model.\ntick: 4\n```"
