  The generalization requirement was a glaring blind spot. I need to showcase AABBG''s ability to be applied *in a new context*. The reviewer''s suggestion to use Combat correction is perfect, and surprisingly, I *already* have the data from previous experiments (ID 1271). I''ll reframe that result as the generalization test I need.


  This means I now have a solid set of experiments: A SOTA replication, a global baseline, the pure AABBG graph, the standard AABBG embedding, the k-parameter test, and this new Combat-corrected application. That''s *more* than enough to make my case.


  The length is the biggest challenge; I need to be ruthlessly concise. I''ll get rid of the verbose explanations and long metric definitions. My core message is AABBG. How does it work? How well does it perform, *particularly* on local batch mixing? And how does it compare?


  The revised plan is straightforward: a tight abstract, a concise introduction, a detailed but focused method section on AABBG (forgetting any of the system-related discussions), a rigorous experimental evaluation section with a *focused* number of experiments, and a pared-down results and discussion section. The conclusion will simply reiterate the method''s value.


  The word count has to be around 500-700 words, and every word counts. I need to be disciplined. I''ll set up a new capsule immediately. This time, I *will* get this right.

