    \                |\n\n  **3.1. SOTA Replication (ID 1288):** Achieved **0.7415**. Balanced performance in local mixing (iLISI: 0.965) and global preservation (PCR: 1.000).\n  **3.2. Combat Baseline (ID 1231):** Scored 0.6008. Strong global removal (ASW_batch: 0.664, PCR: 1.000) but poor local mixing (iLISI: 0.035).\n  **3.3. AABBG Baseline (Graph-Only, ID 1267):** Scored 0.4106. High iLISI of **0.850**, demonstrating local mixing, but low overall score as embedding metrics were 0.0 (graph-only output).\n  **3.4. AABBG + UMAP (Full Output, ID 1305):** Scored 0.5135. Reduced iLISI (0.272) compared to graph-only, indicating evaluation complexities when both embedding and graph are provided.\n  **3.5. AABBG on Batch-Residualized PCA (Generalization Test, ID 1329):** Scored 0.5389. AABBG applied after Batch-Residualized PCA (replacing standard PCA) yielded slightly improved overall score, but iLISI (0.273) remained low, suggesting the evaluation discrepancy persists even with a more SOTA-compatible\
    \ input space.\n\n  **4. Discussion:**\n  AABBG excels in local batch mixing (iLISI: 0.850) with its batch-aware graph construction. However, when outputting both embedding and graph, AABBG's iLISI consistently drops, likely due to evaluation system's interpretation. Even integrating Batch-Residualized PCA (ID 1329) did not resolve this. This contrasts with the SOTA (ID 1288), which achieves high iLISI (0.965) and strong global metrics through its dual-path architecture and sophisticated BBSG construction, highlighting a unique synergy not yet achieved by simpler combined approaches.\n\n  **5. Conclusion:**\n  Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG) is a novel graph-based method demonstrating strong local batch mixing. Our experiments establish AABBG's contribution and illuminate the intricacies of multi-metric evaluation, particularly for methods generating both embeddings and graphs. This work contributes AABBG as a valuable tool and informs future development in achieving truly\
