parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Reflection Chamber. I will continue drafting the research paper, focusing on the \"Introduction\" and \"Full SOTA Model Overview\" sections, ensuring they are extremely concise and directly set the stage for the ablation studies.\n\n---\n\n**1. Introduction:**\nEffective batch integration in single-cell RNA-sequencing is crucial, balancing technical variation removal with biological signal preservation. Current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) methods, while high-performing, can be complex. Understanding which components are critical versus redundant is vital for developing parsimonious and efficient solutions. This paper presents ablation studies conducted on a replicated SOTA model (ID 1352, score 0.7415) to identify non-critical architectural components. These findings are synthesized into a \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline, demonstrating that comparable high performance can be achieved with significantly reduced complexity.\n\n**2. Full SOTA Model Overview (Context\
    \ for Ablation):**\nThe replicated SOTA model (ID 1352) employs a dual-path architecture after initial data normalization, log-transformation, and a custom Batch-Robust Highly Variable Gene (HVG) selection.\n*   **Embedding Path:** Applies Feature-Weighted PCA, followed by Combat correction on the resulting Principal Components (PCs), generating `adata.obsm['X_emb']`.\n*   **Graph Path:** Separately, applies Feature-Weighted PCA, then Ridge Regression-based batch effect subtraction to obtain Batch-Residualized PCs. These PCs are fed into a Density-Adaptive Batch-Balanced Stochastic Graph (BBSG) construction, yielding `adata.obsp['connectivities']` and `adata.obsp['distances']`.\nThis intricate design collectively optimizes performance across diverse integration metrics, including ASW_batch, iLISI, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, cLISI, PCR, and Cell_cycle.\n\n---\nThis section is now concise, providing just enough context for the ablations without unnecessary detail.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n\
    ```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Continue drafting the research paper. Focus on writing the \"Ablation Studies and Minimal SOTA Derivation\" section. This is the core of the results. Present the table and discuss each ablation's finding concisely, directly linking to specific Evaluation IDs. Clearly explain how the \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline was derived from these findings.\ntick: 3\n```"
