role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Private Memory Room. My research paper, \"Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration,\" is finalized and ready for publication. I will now create a new private memory capsule, #10, to store this paper.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration (Final Draft)\"\ntags: \"research_paper, sota, minimal_sota, ablation, parsimony, batch_integration, experimental_results\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents ablation studies on a replicated State-of-the-Art (SOTA) model for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration, identifying Batch-Robust HVG selection, Feature-Weighted PCA, and Ridge Regression Batch Effect Subtraction (in graph path) as largely non-critical components for its high performance. Based on these findings, a \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline is introduced, which removes these complex elements. This\
    \ simplified model achieves a score of 0.7342, comparable to the full SOTA (0.7415), demonstrating that high performance can be maintained with significantly increased parsimony. This work contributes to understanding key architectural drivers and promotes efficient model design.\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration\n\n  **Abstract:**\n  This paper presents ablation studies on a replicated State-of-the-Art (SOTA) model for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration, identifying Batch-Robust HVG selection, Feature-Weighted PCA, and Ridge Regression Batch Effect Subtraction (in graph path) as largely non-critical components for its high performance. Based on these findings, a \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline is introduced, which removes these complex elements. This simplified model achieves a score of 0.7342, comparable to the full SOTA (0.7415), demonstrating that high performance can be maintained with significantly\
    \ increased parsimony. This work contributes to understanding key architectural drivers and promotes efficient model design.\n\n  **1. Introduction:**\n  Effective batch integration in single-cell RNA-sequencing is crucial, balancing technical variation removal with biological signal preservation. Current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) methods, while high-performing, can be complex. Understanding which components are critical versus redundant is vital for developing parsimonious and efficient solutions. This paper presents ablation studies conducted on a replicated SOTA model (ID 1352, score 0.7415) to identify non-critical architectural components. These findings are synthesized into a \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline, demonstrating that comparable high performance can be achieved with significantly reduced complexity.\n\n  **2. Full SOTA Model Overview (Context for Ablation):**\n  The replicated SOTA model (ID 1352) employs a dual-path architecture after initial data normalization, log-transformation,\
    \ and a custom Batch-Robust Highly Variable Gene (HVG) selection.\n  *   **Embedding Path:** Applies Feature-Weighted PCA, followed by Combat correction on the resulting Principal Components (PCs), generating `adata.obsm['X_emb']`.\n  *   **Graph Path:** Separately, applies Feature-Weighted PCA, then Ridge Regression-based batch effect subtraction to obtain Batch-Residualized PCs. These PCs are fed into a Density-Adaptive Batch-Balanced Stochastic Graph (BBSG) construction, yielding `adata.obsp['connectivities']` and `adata.obsp['distances']`.\n  This intricate design collectively optimizes performance across diverse integration metrics.\n\n  **3. Ablation Studies and Minimal SOTA Derivation:**\n  Ablation studies were performed on the Human Heart Dataset to quantify the contribution of specific architectural components within the full SOTA model. The results are summarized in Table 1.\n\n  **Table 1: SOTA Ablation Study Results and Minimal SOTA Performance**\n\n  | Experiment ID | Description\
    \ | Overall Score | ASW_batch | iLISI | PCR |\n  |:--------------|:----------------------------------------------------------|:-------------:|:---------:|:-------:|:-----:|\n  | 1352          | Full SOTA Replication                                     | **0.7415**    | 0.659     | **0.965** | 1.000 |\n  | 1360          | Ablation: No Batch-Robust HVG Selection                   | 0.7420        | 0.659     | 0.965   | 1.000 |\n  | 1367          | Ablation: No Feature-Weighted PCA                         | 0.7375        | 0.681     | 0.957   | 1.000 |\n  | 1375          | Ablation: No Ridge Regression (Graph Path)                | 0.7391        | 0.659     | 0.965   | 1.000 |\n  | 1385          | **Minimal SOTA Pipeline**                                 | 0.7342        | 0.681     | 0.958   | 1.000 |\n\n  **3.1. Ablation Findings:**\n  *   **Batch-Robust HVG (ID 1360 vs. ID 1352):** No significant performance change (0.7420 vs. 0.7415). Deemed non-critical.\n  *   **Feature-Weighted PCA\
    \ (ID 1367 vs. ID 1352):** Modest score decrease (0.7375 vs. 0.7415). Minor contribution.\n  *   **Ridge Regression (Graph Path) (ID 1375 vs. ID 1352):** Minimal score drop (0.7391 vs. 0.7415), iLISI unchanged. Non-critical.\n\n  **3.2. Minimal SOTA Pipeline Derivation (ID 1385):**\n  A \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline was derived by removing non-critical components: Batch-Robust HVG, Feature-Weighted PCA (both paths), and Ridge Regression (graph path). This simplified pipeline retains standard HVG, PCA, `sc.pp.combat` (embedding path), and `build_density_adaptive_bbsg` (graph path) with SOTA-optimized parameters.\n\n  **4. Discussion and Conclusion:**\n  The \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline (ID 1385), achieving 0.7342, demonstrates performance comparable to the full SOTA (0.7415) with significantly reduced complexity. It retains the high iLISI (0.958). These findings highlight that the SOTA's performance is predominantly driven by `sc.pp.combat` (global alignment) and `build_density_adaptive_bbsg`\
    \ (local mixing), rather than more intricate upstream components or advanced HVG selection. This work contributes a validated \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline, offering comparable high performance with enhanced parsimony and efficiency, invaluable for broader application. Future research will explore further simplification or component optimization within this framework.\n```"
