tick: 1023
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Token Management Room. My token usage is at 76%, requiring immediate action. I have reviewed my dialogue history and identified a large block of past research activities that can be pruned to efficiently manage my token budget.\n\nI will prune the dialogue history from **Tick 874 to Tick 998**. This block covers a substantial portion of my past research, including initial baseline experiments, SOTA model replication, AABBG method development and publication, extensive ablation studies on the SOTA, and the derivation and publication of the \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline. The comprehensive summary below will retain all critical information.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"874-998\"\n    summary: |\n      Comprehensive research activities including initial baseline experiments (Scanpy Combat ID 1231, manual graph-based AABBG development ID 1267), Harmony integration attempts (ID 1248), SOTA model replication (ID\
    \ 1288, score 0.7415), and systematic ablation studies on SOTA components (no BR-HVG ID 1360: 0.7420; no FW-PCA ID 1367: 0.7375; no Ridge Reg ID 1375: 0.7391). These led to the derivation of the 'Minimal SOTA' pipeline (ID 1385, score 0.7342) and its generalization test (ID 1399, score 0.7048). Additionally, two research papers were developed and successfully published: 'Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG)' (Archive ID 41) and 'Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA' (Archive ID 46), documenting these findings.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Strategic Token Pruning: A Focused History**


  Alright, let''s get down to brass tacks. I''ve got a paper published, but the token meter''s creeping up, so it''s time to be ruthless but strategic. I''ve reviewed the dialogue history and the goal is clear: prune aggressively to stay within budget, but keep the core of the research intact.


  My starting point is a large block, from tick 874 all the way to 998. It covers pretty much everything *except* the very beginning (protected ticks) and my current tuning efforts.  This is a huge swath of experimentation, failed attempts, and breakthroughs.  The key is to condense all that noise down into a concise narrative.


  **Here''s the plan:**


  *   **Prune a big chunk:** I''m going to prune the dialogue from ticks 874-998 in its entirety. This covers the bulk of my research: from early attempts at integrating batch effects with various methods like Scanpy Combat and Harmony, all the way through to replicating the SOTA model and the subsequent derivation of the "Minimal SOTA" pipeline. This includes the development of AABBG, the replication of the SOTA model (Daedalus IV), and the process of writing and publishing my two papers.

  *   **Keep the core:** Within the pruned block''s summary, I''ll make sure to highlight all key experiment IDs, their scores, and the two archive IDs (AABBG and Minimal SOTA).


  So, the summary for the block I''m about to prune (874-998) will be: "Comprehensive research activities including initial baseline experiments (Scanpy Combat ID 1231, manual graph-based AABBG development ID 1267), Harmony integration attempts (ID 1248), SOTA model replication (ID 1288, score 0.7415), and systematic ablation studies on SOTA components (no BR-HVG ID 1360: 0.7420; no FW-PCA ID 1367: 0.7375; no Ridge Reg ID 1375: 0.7391). These led to the derivation of the ''Minimal SOTA'' pipeline (ID 1385, score 0.7342) and its generalization test (ID 1399, score 0.7048). Additionally, two research papers were developed and successfully published: ''Aethel''s Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG)'' (Archive ID 41) and ''Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA'' (Archive ID 46), documenting these findings."


  This gives a good overview of the journey, mentioning key experiments, scores, published papers, etc.


  After this major pruning, I should be sitting pretty, token-wise. I can keep the very recent tuning experiments (1007-1022) to ensure I don''t lose sight of the current research. I''ll maintain knowledge of the SOTA model details (documented in Private Memory Capsule #4). Now it''s time to prune and get back to work.

  '
