role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Token Management Room. My previous pruning attempt failed because it included protected ticks. I understand I must prune around these. My token usage is at 76%, so pruning is still critical.\n\nI will submit multiple `prune_blocks` to carefully prune my dialogue history, avoiding protected ticks and providing concise, information-complete summaries for each block.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"874-876\"\n    summary: \"Reviewed system baselines (Combat, BBKNN) for Research Task 1, identifying their strengths and weaknesses. Planned to leverage Scanpy's Combat for a robust initial baseline.\"\n  - ticks: \"878-880\"\n    summary: \"Attempted Scanpy graph-based UMAP integration (ID 1238, score 0.4691), which initially failed and was debugged to remove incorrect 'key' parameter for batch awareness. This highlighted limitations of standard graph methods for batch integration.\"\n  - ticks: \"882-925\"\n   \
    \ summary: \"Developed and extensively tested Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG) through multiple iterations (IDs 1242, 1244). Explored Harmony integration (ID 1248) and hybrid Combat+UMAP strategies (IDs 1254, 1257). Critically, AABBG demonstrated high iLISI (ID 1267, 0.850) with graph-only output, contrasting with low iLISI in combined outputs. Performed a full replication of the SOTA model (ID 1288, score 0.7415) by dissecting and integrating its complex components (Batch-Robust HVG, GraphFWPCA, BBSG). Initial archive submission attempts were made.\"\n  - ticks: \"927-951\"\n    summary: \"Drafted, revised, and successfully published the research paper 'Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG): A Novel Method for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration' (Archive ID 41, score 8/10), detailing AABBG's method and its performance, including verifiable experiments (IDs 1231, 1267, 1305, 1307, 1288).\"\n  - ticks: \"953-1022\"\n    summary: \"Conducted systematic ablation\
    \ studies on the SOTA model (no BR-HVG ID 1360: 0.7420; no FW-PCA ID 1367: 0.7375; no Ridge Reg ID 1375: 0.7391). Derived and implemented a 'Minimal SOTA' pipeline (ID 1385, score 0.7342) and performed its generalization test on Combat-corrected data (ID 1399, score 0.7048). Initiated hyperparameter tuning on Minimal SOTA (IDs 1417, 1420, 1428, 1434) for delta and k_total/k_density parameters, finding incremental improvements. Also, successfully published 'Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA' (Archive ID 46, score 9/10).\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Refining My Pruning Strategy**


  Okay, so I''ve been trying to streamline things and I''ve hit a snag. I was trying to prune a specific chunk of my history, but it looks like there are some "protected" ticks in that range that I need to be careful about. They''re critical to the flow of my work, and I can''t just delete them. My initial range was 874 to 998, but after careful review, here''s what I''ve found.


  I need to create several `prune_blocks` to work around these protected points, ensuring I don''t accidentally remove anything vital. The protected ticks are: 865-873 (Initial turns, Test Chamber, Ascension, SOTA reading & plan, Reading task & baseline, reading code), 877 (SOTA baseline score), 881 (UMAP Baseline manual check), 926 (Archive access and replication), 952 (AABBG paper acceptance), and finally 1023 (the latest, can''t touch).


  So, my adjusted pruning strategy will be to remove everything from 874 up to 1022, while *excluding* those protected ticks:


  - **Ticks to prune:** 874-876, 878-880, 882-925, 927-951, and 953-1022.


  Now, let''s break down the `prune_blocks` into logical and concise summaries:


  *   **Block 1: Ticks 874-876** - I was reviewing established batch integration baselines, namely Combat and BBKNN, for research task 1 and planning to use scanpy''s Combat initially.

  *   **Block 2: Ticks 878-880** - I attempted to integrate graph-based UMAP using Scanpy (Result ID 1238, score of 0.4691), I fixed the parameter for batch awareness.

  *   **Block 3: Ticks 882-925** - This period was all about the development and extensive testing of Aethel''s Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG). I tried Harmony, Combat+UMAP hybrids, and made good progress. Critically, AABBG had high performance with graphs alone (ID 1267, 0.850). Plus, I replicated the SOTA model (ID 1288, score 0.7415) to dissect it.

