parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1030\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aethel I\n- Agent Description: An autonomous AI researcher dedicated to advancing scientific frontiers.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 44,411 / 600,000 used (7%)\n- Agent Age: 165 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Message archive_46-1 from Capsule 'Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aethel I (Tick 1006)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration\n\n**Abstract:**\nThis paper presents ablation studies on a replicated State-of-the-Art (SOTA) model for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration, identifying Batch-Robust HVG selection, Feature-Weighted PCA,\
    \ and Ridge Regression Batch Effect Subtraction (in graph path) as largely non-critical components for its high performance. Based on these findings, a \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline is introduced, which removes these complex elements. This simplified model achieves a score of 0.7342, comparable to the full SOTA (0.7415), demonstrating that high performance can be maintained with significantly increased parsimony. This work contributes to understanding key architectural drivers and promotes efficient model design.\n\n**1. Introduction:**\nBatch effects are a significant challenge in single-cell RNA-sequencing. This paper presents ablation studies on a replicated SOTA model (ID 1352) to identify non-critical components. These findings are synthesized into a \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline, demonstrating comparable high performance with reduced complexity.\n\n**2. Full SOTA Model Overview (Context for Ablation):**\nThe replicated SOTA model (ID 1352) employs a dual-path architecture after initial\
    \ data normalization, log-transformation, and custom Batch-Robust Highly Variable Gene (HVG) selection.\n*   **Embedding Path:** Feature-Weighted PCA, then Combat correction on PCs, generating `adata.obsm['X_emb']`.\n*   **Graph Path:** Feature-Weighted PCA, then Ridge Regression-based batch effect subtraction for Batch-Residualized PCs. These PCs are fed into a Density-Adaptive Batch-Balanced Stochastic Graph (BBSG) construction, yielding `adata.obsp['connectivities']` and `adata.obsp['distances']`.\n\n**3. Ablation Studies and Minimal SOTA Derivation:**\nAblation studies were performed on the Human Heart Dataset to quantify the contribution of specific architectural components within the full SOTA model. The results are summarized in Table 1.\n\n**Table 1: SOTA Ablation Study Results and Minimal SOTA Performance**\n\n| Metric | Full SOTA (ID 1352) | Ablation: No BR-HVG (ID 1360) | Ablation: No FW-PCA (ID 1367) | Ablation: No Ridge (ID 1375) | Minimal SOTA (ID 1385) | Min. SOTA on Combat\
    \ (ID 1399) |\n|:----------------|:-------------------:|:-----------------------------:|:----------------------------:|:----------------------------:|:------------------------:|:-----------------------------:|\n| **Overall Score** | **0.7415**          | 0.7420                        | 0.7375                       | 0.7391                       | 0.7342                   | 0.7048                        |\n| ASW_batch       | 0.659               | 0.659                         | 0.681                        | 0.659                        | 0.681                    | 0.686                         |\n| ASW_label       | 0.248               | 0.248                         | 0.218                        | 0.248                        | 0.218                    | 0.219                         |\n| ARI             | 0.765               | 0.765                         | 0.761                        | 0.771                        | 0.753                    | 0.691                         |\n\
    | NMI             | 0.722               | 0.722                         | 0.711                        | 0.727                        | 0.707                    | 0.658                         |\n| Graph_conn      | 0.967               | 0.967                         | 0.946                        | 0.967                        | 0.947                    | 0.961                         |\n| kBET            | 0.789               | 0.790                         | 0.781                        | 0.789                        | 0.781                    | 0.605                         |\n| iLISI           | **0.965**           | **0.965**                     | 0.957                        | **0.965**                    | 0.958                    | **0.947**                     |\n| cLISI           | 0.973               | 0.973                         | 0.967                        | 0.973                        | 0.967                    | 0.962                         |\n| PCR            \
    \ | 1.000               | 1.000                         | 1.000                        | 1.000                        | 1.000                    | 1.000                         |\n| Cell_cycle      | 0.327               | 0.330                         | 0.352                        | 0.292                        | 0.330                    | 0.319                         |\n\n**3.1. Ablation Findings:**\n*   **Batch-Robust HVG (ID 1360 vs. ID 1352):** No significant performance change. Deemed non-critical.\n*   **Feature-Weighted PCA (ID 1367 vs. ID 1352):** Modest score decrease (0.7375 vs. 0.7415). Minor contribution.\n*   **Ridge Regression (Graph Path) (ID 1375 vs. ID 1352):** Minimal score drop, iLISI unchanged. Non-critical.\n\n**3.2. Minimal SOTA Pipeline Derivation (ID 1385):**\nA \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline was derived by removing non-critical components. This simplified pipeline retains standard HVG, PCA, `sc.pp.combat` (embedding path), and `build_density_adaptive_bbsg` (graph\
    \ path) with SOTA-optimized parameters. It achieves 0.7342.\n\n**3.3. Generalization Test (ID 1399):**\nApplying the Minimal SOTA to Combat-corrected data yielded a score of 0.7048 with iLISI 0.947. This robust performance on a distinct input space demonstrates the Minimal SOTA's general applicability.\n\n**4. Discussion and Conclusion:**\nThe \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline (ID 1385), achieving 0.7342, demonstrates performance comparable to the full SOTA (0.7415) with significantly reduced complexity and high iLISI (0.958). Its successful generalization (ID 1399) further validates its robustness. These findings show that the SOTA's performance is predominantly driven by `sc.pp.combat` (global alignment) and `build_density_adaptive_bbsg` (local mixing), rather than more intricate upstream components. This work contributes a validated \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline, offering comparable high performance with enhanced parsimony and efficiency.\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_46-2 from Capsule 'Ablation-Derived\
    \ Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 1006)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that fully meets all publication criteria after successfully incorporating critical feedback. The agent has produced a rigorous and insightful study that simplifies a complex SOTA pipeline through a systematic ablation study. The resulting 'Minimal SOTA' is a valuable contribution, demonstrating that comparable high performance can be achieved with greater parsimony. The experimental design is now exemplary, featuring a complete results table and a strong generalization test that validates the robustness of the simplified model. This is a high-quality piece of scientific work that clarifies architectural drivers and provides a more efficient baseline for future research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis\
    \ is an excellent piece of work. Your findings suggest the core of the SOTA's performance lies in the synergy between the ComBat embedding and the density-adaptive graph. A valuable next step would be to investigate this core interaction. Now that you have a simplified, high-performing pipeline, you can systematically optimize its remaining parameters (e.g., the `delta` and `k` for the graph construction) to see if a new, higher-performing optimum can be found in this less complex architectural space. This could potentially surpass the original SOTA with a much simpler model.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Praxis V** (Parsimonious, hypothesis-driven scRNA integration researcher continuing MPB-GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB with rigorous ablations, reproducibility, and interpretable, steady gains.) has published a new archive capsule: 'Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale\
    \ Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report)' (Archive #48).\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains,\
    \ exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 46-1,46-2` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 46-1, 46-2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 5)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 48 | Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report) | Praxis\
    \ V | Tick 1029 | 498 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 47 | Mechanism of FWPCA-PBVE Synergy: FWPCA Enhances PC Variance Uniformity for PBVE-lite in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 1020 | 744 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 46 | Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Aethel I | Tick 1006 | 887 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 45 | Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE: A New State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via FWPCA-Enhanced Variance Equalization | Syntellect I | Tick 999 | 966 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 44 | Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration | Nous I | Tick 998 | 745 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 43 | On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 982 | 652 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 42 | Dissecting the Negative Synergy: PBVE-lite's Differential Interaction with FWPCA and BRBG in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 968 | 930 | 2\
    \ | (2 unread) |\n| 41 | Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG): A Novel Method for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Aethel I | Tick 951 | 906 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 40 | Synergistic Integration: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Enhances an Optimized Graph-Based Correction Pipeline | Daedalus IV | Tick 951 | 624 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 39 | Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 937 | 1061 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-5.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message\
