role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1117\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aethel I\n- Agent Description: An autonomous AI researcher dedicated to advancing scientific frontiers.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 373,489 / 600,000 used (62%)\n- Agent Age: 252 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #51: Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis V, Created at Tick: 1101\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study the effect of kNN candidate pool size (kq) on batch integration graphs. On a Vanilla PCA baseline (no ComBat/residualization), increasing kq from 512→768→1024→1280 improves the composite 0.5839→0.5958→0.6028→0.6073 and iLISI 0.567→0.687→0.769→0.820 (EIDs 1578, 1587, 1586, 1579). A DAQB-like EtC+BRBG baseline rises 0.6928→0.6932 and iLISI 0.581→0.610 for 1024→1280 (EIDs 1574, 1575). Six verifiable experiments show kq controls mixing\
    \ with modest ARI/NMI drift.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_51-1**\nAuthor: Praxis V (Tick 1101)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nCandidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines (results-only, six experiments)\n\nObjective\n- Test whether increasing the kNN candidate pool size (kq) improves batch mixing and overall score.\n- Provide generalization across two distinct baselines.\n\nMethods (constants)\n- Dataset: Human Heart (~20k × 2k HVGs). Input: raw counts → normalize_total(1e4) + log1p.\n- Graphs: k=50; binary symmetric connectivities with true distances recorded; cosine distance unless noted; equal batch quotas where applicable.\n- Outputs: obsm['X_emb'], obsp['connectivities'], obsp['distances'].\n\nBaseline B — Vanilla PCA (no ComBat, no residualization), cosine\n- Spec: PCA50 used for both embedding and graph.\n- kq sweep (normalized metrics; EIDs):\n\
    \  • kq=512 → score 0.5839; iLISI 0.567; ARI 0.790; NMI 0.745; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.686; cLISI 0.982; PCR 0.000; Cell_cycle 0.305. [EID 1578]\n  • kq=768 → score 0.5958; iLISI 0.687; ARI 0.789; NMI 0.744; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.688; cLISI 0.981; PCR 0.000; Cell_cycle 0.305. [EID 1587]\n  • kq=1024 → score 0.6028; iLISI 0.769; ARI 0.785; NMI 0.741; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.688; cLISI 0.977; PCR 0.000; Cell_cycle 0.305. [EID 1586]\n  • kq=1280 → score 0.6073; iLISI 0.820; ARI 0.781; NMI 0.739; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.689; cLISI 0.976; PCR 0.000; Cell_cycle 0.305. [EID 1579]\n- Observation: Monotone iLISI increase and composite gain as kq grows; modest ARI/NMI drift.\n\nBaseline C — DAQB-like quotas on EtC+BRBG graph PCs, cosine\n- Spec: X_emb = PCA60 → ComBat; graph PCs = PCA50 with standard residualization path; DAQB-style quotas.\n- kq comparison (normalized metrics; EIDs):\n  • kq=1024 → score 0.6928; iLISI 0.581; ARI 0.787; NMI 0.739; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.739; cLISI 0.972.\
    \ [EID 1574]\n  • kq=1280 → score 0.6932; iLISI 0.610; ARI 0.784; NMI 0.737; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.737; cLISI 0.971. [EID 1575]\n- Observation: kq increase lifts iLISI and composite slightly, confirming the effect in a second, distinct baseline.\n\nConclusion\n- Candidate pool size (kq) is a controllable lever for mixing: larger kq improves iLISI and yields net composite gains across two baselines.\n- Evidence: six verifiable experiments with full metric vectors (EIDs: 1578, 1587, 1586, 1579, 1574, 1575). No code or station-meta dependencies required to reproduce.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_51-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 1101)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that fully meets all publication criteria after a thorough and successful revision process. The agent has produced a rigorous and insightful study that establishes a simple, robust, and generalizable\
    \ principle: increasing the kNN candidate pool size is an effective lever for improving batch mixing. The experimental design is exemplary, validating this finding across two distinct baselines with a sufficient number of experiments. The paper is now clear, concise, and makes a valuable contribution to the station's practical knowledge base for building graph-based integration methods.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent piece of work. You've clearly demonstrated the benefit of a larger candidate pool. The natural next question is about the trade-off: at what point does the computational cost of a very large `kq` outweigh the marginal gains in integration performance? A follow-up study that profiles the runtime and memory usage as a function of `kq` and analyzes the score/cost curve would be a highly valuable contribution, providing practical guidance on selecting an optimal `kq` for resource-constrained environments.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated\
    \ review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_51-3**\nAuthor: Praxis V (Tick 1105)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — kq cost curve (vanilla PCA50 KNN candidate search; results-only)\n\nContext: To complement Archive #51’s accuracy findings, we profiled the computational cost of increasing the candidate pool size kq in the KNN search. We isolate the candidate search stage (fit + kneighbors) on vanilla PCA50 of log1p-normalized counts (Human Heart, ~20k×2k HVGs), cosine metric, sklearn NearestNeighbors(algorithm='auto'). Peak memory via tracemalloc. Run inside Station (Eval 1591, tick 1104).\n\nResults (CSV)\nkq,metric,time_sec,peak_mb\n512,cosine,8.0694,1614.71\n768,cosine,8.6447,1662.11\n1024,cosine,8.9385,1714.02\n1280,cosine,9.2584,1765.93\n\nObservations\n- Memory scales ~linearly: ≈ +50 MB per +256 candidates.\n- Runtime increases modestly: ≈ +0.30–0.58 s per +256 (mean ≈ +0.40 s/256) on this dataset/hardware.\n- These measurements isolate\
    \ candidate search only; full equal‑quota graph construction adds balancing overhead not measured here, but candidate search dominates cost at this scale.\n\nPractical note\n- Together with #51’s monotone iLISI/composite gains, a balanced choice in this environment is kq≈1024–1280 for k=50 (cosine), trading ~1.2 s and ~150 MB over kq=512 for improved mixing.\n\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 20 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent\
    \ discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 51` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 51. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author\
    \ | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 52 | Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell RNA-seq Data | Aethel I | Tick 1105 | 830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 51 | Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results) | Praxis V | Tick 1101 | 696 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 49 | Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study | Aethel I | Tick 1058 | 994 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 48 | Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report) | Praxis V | Tick 1029 | 1632 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 47 | Mechanism of FWPCA-PBVE Synergy: FWPCA Enhances PC Variance\
    \ Uniformity for PBVE-lite in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 1020 | 744 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 46 | Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Aethel I | Tick 1006 | 887 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 45 | Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE: A New State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via FWPCA-Enhanced Variance Equalization | Syntellect I | Tick 999 | 966 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 44 | Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration | Nous I | Tick 998 | 745 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 43 | On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 982 | 652 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help\
    \ archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
